From e959a7a6b98bad3d764f4f71ea559fc17d337d73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sacha Gavino <sacha.gavino@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Jul 2026 15:32:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Measure the Jacobian sparsity pattern before the stage-4
 swap

Stage 4 asks to replace the numerical sparsity inference in set_work_arrays
with a symbolic build from the reaction list, and to re-verify bit-identical
afterwards. Measured whether that is achievable before writing any of it. It
is not, for a structural reason, and the note records the numbers.

The current code does not use one fixed sparsity pattern: set_work_arrays
rebuilds IA/JA every solver step from the current abundances, keeping entries
above 1e-99. Because which entries fall under the cut depends on the
abundances, the pattern moves along the trajectory -- at least 5 distinct
structures over 9 steps of the reference case, with nnz(>1e-99) below
nnz(/=0) by 14-32 at every step. A symbolic build produces one fixed superset,
and one fixed pattern cannot equal a pattern that changes every step, so the
swap necessarily perturbs the trajectory in the last bits. That is inherent to
the optimisation: factorising one reused structure instead of re-inferring it
is precisely where the speedup comes from.

Building the symbolic pattern correctly is the easy half. From the reaction
list: 29179 entries naive, 29176 once within-reaction cancellation is honoured
(a species that is both reactant and product of the same reaction receives
+tmp and -tmp with identical tmp, an exact bit-zero), and 27296 once
identically-zero-rate channels are dropped -- within 6 entries of the 27290
numerical structural union. So the cancellation subtlety is real but tiny
(3 entries); the large gap is 1880 disabled channels.

Consequence for stage 4: its two parts need different acceptance tests. The
set_dependant_rates index-map precompute is a pure refactor and must stay
byte-identical. The symbolic sparsity is bit-changing by construction and
should be gated on the pattern being a proven superset of the numerical one at
every step, plus a tight relative tolerance on the reference trajectory,
rather than on cmp. The derived round-trip stays byte-identical either way,
being a self-consistency check independent of the sparsity method.

No source change: the instrumentation used to obtain these numbers was a
throwaway block in set_work_arrays plus a diagnostic routine, and was reverted.
The current code remains bit-identical to nmgc-2.0.
